        "Evaluate the sign of the derivative:\nSince $(x^2 - 3)^2 \\ge 0$ for all real $x$, we have $f'(x) \\ge 5 > 0$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\nBecause the first derivative is strictly positive everywhere, $f$ is an **increasing function**."
